# Built command binaries land in the repository ROOT, named after the command:
# `go build ./cmd/docscan/` writes `./docscan`. Nothing ignored them, and
# `git add -A` duly committed one -- a 3.8 MB arm64 Mach-O executable was
# tracked on `main` from #11 onwards, and `go build ./cmd/truncspill/` staged a
# second one before this file existed.
#
# This is an ALLOW-LIST, deliberately, rather than a list of binary names. A
# rule that names the binaries it has already met goes stale at the first
# rename and at every new command -- and the artefact nobody has a rule for is
# exactly the one that walks in. Verify BOTH directions after changing it:
#
#     git check-ignore -v docscan        # must be ignored
#     git check-ignore -v README.md      # must print nothing, exit 1
#
/*
!/*/
!/.gitignore
!/README.md
!/go.mod
!/go.sum
